  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "GgXG38nY7JEPLXUp13kI5NS60WI=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:24:45.290Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "CiviCRM-class membership tooling stores memberships, donations and event registrations as rows that are updated in place. The research adds the detail that decides the case: the logging module is optional and thin, so even where it is switched on the trail is a side-channel rather than the system's spine.\n\nThe consequence is that the membership lifecycle \u2014 joined, lapsed, reinstated, upgraded, refunded \u2014 can be read as a current state but not reconstructed as a history. For an organisation whose obligations follow from that history, a row is the wrong shape of evidence: it says what is true now and is silent about when it became true and who made it so.\n\nSYSTOPIA's business is a delivery practice built on this tooling, which places the gap at a layer they already control. The move is not a replacement CRM but an auditable record sitting under the lifecycle events the CRM already produces, which keeps the proposal additive to the practice they sell.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**1 \u00b7 start here**\n- SYSTOPIA GmbH (DE Bonn) \u2014 Fabian Schuttenberg + Martin Peth, Gesch\u00e4ftsf\u00fchrer \u00b7 info@systopia.de \u00b7 https://systopia.de\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> CiviCRM membership, donation and event data are mutable rows; the logging module is optional and thin. Powerhouse gives the membership lifecycle an auditable spine.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:24:45.290799Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:24:45.290804Z"
    }
  },
